from . import n
n("solar_system_formation","Formation of the Solar System","9",(1e9,1e13),"C2","Planetary science",
 "The Sun and planets formed about 4.57 billion years ago from a collapsing cloud of gas and dust. Dust stuck together into planetesimals, then planets.",
 "Rocky planets formed near the Sun where it was too hot for ice; giants formed beyond the snow line. Giant planets may have migrated, as in the Nice model.",
 "Age 4.568 Gyr (CAIs); snow line ≈2.7 AU; disk mass ≈0.01–0.1 M☉; pebble accretion; Hill radius r_H=a(m/3M)^{1/3}.",
 "Broad picture solid; migration histories debated.",
 em="gravitation_newton rotational_dynamics star_formation",ev="Meteorite ages|Protoplanetary disks (ALMA)|Exoplanet demographics",refs="AST 14; AST 7",ex="planet_types moon_origin_node",fr="Giant-planet migration history (Nice, Grand Tack)~open",sim="solarsystem asteroidbelt")
n("planet_types","Planets: rocky, giant and ice","9",(1e6,1e9),"C1","Planetary science",
 "Terrestrial planets (Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars) are small and rocky; Jupiter and Saturn are gas giants; Uranus and Neptune are ice giants. Each differs in atmosphere, interior and history.",
 "Mass and distance from the Sun decide what a planet can hold. Venus's runaway greenhouse shows how atmosphere can transform a world.",
 "Radii (km): Mercury 2440, Venus 6052, Earth 6371, Mars 3390, Jupiter 69911, Saturn 58232, Uranus 25362, Neptune 24622; Venus surface ≈737 K, 92 bar.",
 "Properties well measured by spacecraft.",
 em="solar_system_formation gravitation_newton",ev="Spacecraft missions",refs="AST 7; AST 8; AST 10; AST 11",sim="planet:mercury planet:venus planet:earth planet:mars planet:jupiter planet:saturn planet:uranus planet:neptune")
n("moon_origin_node","Origin of the Moon","9",(3e6,4e8),"C3","Planetary science",
 "The Moon likely formed when a Mars-sized body hit the young Earth. Lunar rocks are surprisingly similar in isotopes to Earth's, which challenges simple versions of this model.",
 "Alternatives and refinements include a vaporised donut-shaped structure, a faster-spinning Earth, or multiple impacts.",
 "M_Moon=0.0123 M_E; mean distance 384 400 km; Earth-Moon angular momentum 3.5×10³⁴ kg m²/s; Apollo samples ≈4.35–4.5 Gyr.",
 "Giant impact is consensus in outline; details debated.",
 em="solar_system_formation",ev="Apollo samples|Isotope ratios",refs="AST 9; AST 14",fref="moon_origin",sim="moon")
n("earth_water_origin","Where did Earth's water come from?","9",(1e6,1e8),"C3","Planetary science",
 "Water may have arrived with asteroids rich in water, been built into Earth's rocky ingredients, or both. Comets contribute little by their isotopic ratios.",
 "Hydrogen isotope ratios (D/H) compare water in different bodies to Earth's oceans.",
 "Ocean D/H≈1.56×10⁻⁴ (VSMOW); carbonaceous chondrites ≈1.4–1.7×10⁻⁴; comet 67P ≈5.3×10⁻⁴.",
 "Mixed sources likely.",
 em="solar_system_formation water_cycle",ev="D/H measurements",refs="AST 14",fref="earth_water")
n("moons_tidal","Moons, tidal heating and rings","9",(1e3,1e9),"C1","Planetary science",
 "Moons orbiting giant planets are squeezed and flexed by tides. This heats Io into the most volcanic body known and keeps oceans liquid under the ice of Europa and Enceladus.",
 "Giant planets have rings made of ice and rock particles; Saturn's are the most extensive. Titan has a thick atmosphere and methane lakes.",
 "Io heat flow ≈2 W/m²; Enceladus plume ≈200 kg/s; Saturn main rings ≈7×10⁴–1.4×10⁵ km from centre, thickness ≈10 m.",
 "Presence of oceans inferred from gravity/magnetism and plumes.",
 em="planet_types tides",ev="Voyager, Galileo, Cassini",refs="AST 12",sim="moon planet:jupiter planet:saturn")
n("small_bodies","Asteroids, comets and impacts","9",(1,1e14),"C1","Planetary science",
 "Asteroids and comets are leftover building blocks. Asteroids are rocky or metallic, comets icy; both leave craters when they hit planets.",
 "Most asteroids lie between Mars and Jupiter. The Kuiper belt and Oort cloud hold icy bodies. Impacts shaped planetary surfaces; the one 66 Myr ago ended the age of non-avian dinosaurs.",
 "Main belt 2.1–3.3 AU; Ceres 940 km; KE=½mv² (10 km body at 20 km/s ≈10²³ J); Kuiper belt 30–50 AU; Oort cloud ≈2000–10⁵ AU.",
 "Impact rates well constrained for recent times.",
 em="solar_system_formation orbits_kepler",ev="Crater counts|Meteorite recoveries|Sample return (Hayabusa)",refs="AST 13; AST 9",sim="asteroidbelt kuiper oort")
n("exoplanets","Exoplanets","9",(1e6,1e12),"C2","Planetary science",
 "Thousands of planets have been found around other stars using tiny dips in starlight (transits) and star wobbles (radial velocity). Planet types not in our system, like super-Earths, are the most common.",
 "Statistics show most stars host planets. JWST is beginning to read atmospheres of some of them.",
 "Transit depth ≈(R_p/R_*)²; RV semi-amplitude K=28.4 m/s ×(M_p sin i/M_J)(P/1yr)^{-1/3}(M_*/M☉)^{-2/3}.",
 "Detection biases favour large, close-in planets.",
 em="planet_types orbits_kepler",ev="Kepler and TESS surveys|HARPS",refs="AST 21; AST 30",fref="exoplanet_count",sim="exoplanet star")
n("habitability","Habitable zones and astrobiology","9",(1e9,1e13),"C3","Astrobiology",
 "The habitable zone is the range of distances where a planet could have liquid surface water. Life elsewhere is possible but not detected.",
 "Claims of biosignatures, such as gases in atmospheres, need careful testing against non-biological explanations.",
 "HZ inner edge ≈0.95 AU, outer ≈1.4–1.7 AU for Sun-like star; Drake equation N=R*·fp·ne·fl·fi·fc·L.",
 "No confirmed extraterrestrial life.",
 em="exoplanets origin_of_life",ev="Extremophile studies|Planet census",refs="AST 30",fr="Biosignature claims (e.g. phosphine on Venus, DMS on K2-18b) remain unconfirmed~open")
n("sun_structure","The Sun","10",(1e8,1.4e9),"C1","Stellar astrophysics",
 "The Sun is a middle-aged star that shines by fusing hydrogen in its core. Energy takes thousands of years to reach the surface by radiation and convection.",
 "Its surface (photosphere) is about 5800 K, with sunspots, flares and a million-degree corona. The solar wind fills the heliosphere.",
 "L☉=3.828×10²⁶ W; R☉=6.957×10⁸ m; M☉=1.989×10³⁰ kg; T_c≈1.57×10⁷ K; ρ_c≈150 g/cm³; photon diffusion ≈10⁵ yr; 11-yr cycle.",
 "Coronal heating mechanism still debated.",
 em="fusion_stars hydrostatic_balance",ev="Helioseismology|Solar neutrinos|Solar Dynamics Observatory",refs="AST 15; AST 16",sim="sun heliosphere",fr="Coronal heating~open")
n("hydrostatic_balance","Stellar structure and hydrostatic equilibrium","10",(1e7,1e12),"C1","Stellar astrophysics",
 "A star is a balance between gravity pulling inward and pressure pushing outward. Fusion in the core keeps the pressure up.",
 "More massive stars burn hotter and much brighter, so they live shorter lives. This is the mass-luminosity relation.",
 "dP/dr=−Gmρ/r²; L∝M^{3.5} (main sequence); lifetime t≈10 Gyr(M/M☉)^{−2.5}; virial 2K+U=0.",
 "Simplified for spherical, non-rotating stars.",
 em="gravitation_newton kinetic_theory",ev="Mass-luminosity data from binaries",refs="AST 18; AST 22",ex="sun_structure stellar_evolution")
n("stellar_spectra","Stellar spectra and the H-R diagram","10",(1e8,1e12),"C1","Stellar astrophysics",
 "Starlight spectra reveal temperature, composition and motion. Plotting luminosity against temperature groups stars into a main sequence, giants and white dwarfs.",
 "Spectral types O B A F G K M run from hot to cool. Doppler shifts tell speed and binary orbits.",
 "L=4πR²σT⁴; M_V=m−5log(d/10 pc); B−V color index; spectral types O ≳30 000 K to M ≲3 500 K.",
 "Metallicity and rotation modify lines.",
 em="blackbody atomic_spectra",ev="Harvard classification|Hipparcos/Gaia data",refs="AST 17; AST 18",sim="star")
n("star_formation","Star formation and the interstellar medium","10",(1e12,1e18),"C1","Stellar astrophysics",
 "Stars form when cold dense clouds of gas and dust collapse under their own gravity. The space between stars contains gas, dust and cosmic rays.",
 "Collapse needs the cloud to be massive enough (Jeans mass). Protostars grow with disks and jets.",
 "Jeans mass M_J≈(5kT/Gμm_H)^{3/2}(3/4πρ)^{1/2}; molecular cloud T≈10 K, n≈10²–10⁶ cm⁻³; star formation efficiency ≈1–10 %.",
 "Role of magnetic fields and turbulence still studied.",
 em="gravitation_newton states_of_matter",ev="Infrared and radio observations of Orion|ALMA disks",refs="AST 20; AST 21",ex="solar_system_formation stellar_evolution",sim="neighborhood region")
n("stellar_evolution","Stellar evolution","10",(1e8,1e13),"C1","Stellar astrophysics",
 "After hydrogen runs out in the core, stars expand into giants. Sun-like stars end as white dwarfs; massive stars explode as supernovae.",
 "Nuclear burning proceeds through helium, carbon and heavier fuel in massive stars, ending with an iron core.",
 "Sun leaves main sequence in ≈5 Gyr; M<8 M☉→white dwarf (<1.4 M☉ Chandrasekhar); M>8 M☉→core-collapse; 25 M☉ lifetime ≈ 7 Myr.",
 "Mass loss, rotation and binarity change tracks.",
 em="hydrostatic_balance fusion_stars",ev="Cluster H-R diagrams",refs="AST 22; AST 23",ex="stellar_death",sim="star")
n("stellar_death","White dwarfs, supernovae and neutron stars","10",(1e4,1e12),"C1","Stellar astrophysics",
 "Dead stars become dense remnants: white dwarfs supported by electron degeneracy, or neutron stars and black holes after core-collapse supernovae.",
 "A teaspoon of neutron-star matter weighs about a billion tonnes. Pulsars are spinning neutron stars with beams.",
 "WD: M≈0.6 M☉, R≈R⊕, M_Ch=1.4 M☉; NS: M≈1.4–2 M☉, R≈11–13 km, ρ≈10¹⁷ kg/m³; SN energy ≈10⁴⁴ J (1 foe).",
 "Neutron-star equation of state uncertain.",
 em="stellar_evolution spin_pauli",ev="Crab pulsar|SN 1987A neutrinos",refs="AST 23; AST 24",ex="black_holes nucleosynthesis_stars",fr="Neutron-star equation of state~open",sim="blackhole star")
n("nucleosynthesis_stars","Origin of the chemical elements","10",(1e-15,1e20),"C2","Astrophysics",
 "The Big Bang made hydrogen, helium and a little lithium; stars forged carbon through iron; most heavier elements are built by capturing neutrons, either slowly (the s-process, in giant stars late in their lives) or rapidly (the r-process, in neutron-rich events such as neutron-star mergers and possibly some supernovae).",
 "We are made of star-processed material. How much of the heaviest elements comes from mergers versus other supernovae is still debated.",
 "Abundance by mass: H 74 %, He 24 %, O 0.8 %, C 0.3 %; BBN D/H≈2.5×10⁻⁵, ⁷Li shortfall ≈3×; r-process peaks A≈80, 130, 195. The s-process in low- and intermediate-mass asymptotic-giant-branch stars and the r-process together produce the bulk of the heavy elements in the solar system (review in IAU Symposium 343, 2019).",
 "Sources of r-process elements debated.",
 em="fusion_stars nuclear_binding stellar_death",ev="Stellar spectra|Meteoritic abundances|GW170817",refs="AST 16; AST 23; AST 29",ex="periodic_table",fr="Neutron-star mergers vs collapsars for r-process~open")
n("kilonova_rprocess","Kilonovae and heavy elements","10",(1e4,1e12),"C2","Astrophysics",
 "When two neutron stars merge, they emit gravitational waves, a burst of gamma rays and a glow powered by freshly made heavy elements such as gold.",
 "GW170817 was seen in both gravitational waves and light, confirming such events create r-process elements.",
 "GW170817: 1.17–1.60 M☉ components, d≈40 Mpc, ejecta ≈0.05 M☉; kilonova peak ≈10⁴¹ erg/s.",
 "Event rate and yields uncertain.",
 em="gravitational_waves nucleosynthesis_stars",ev="GW170817/AT2017gfo",refs="AST 24")
n("binary_variable","Binary and variable stars","10",(1e9,1e13),"C1","Stellar astrophysics",
 "Over half of Sun-like stars have a companion. Variable stars such as Cepheids pulse in a way that reveals their true brightness, used as cosmic distance markers.",
 "Binaries let astronomers weigh stars directly. Type Ia supernovae from white dwarfs in binaries act as standard candles.",
 "Cepheid P-L: M_V≈−2.43(log P−1)−4.05; SN Ia peak M≈−19.3.",
 "Calibration of standard candles underlies the Hubble tension.",
 em="stellar_spectra orbits_kepler",ev="Eclipsing binary masses|Leavitt's law",refs="AST 18; AST 19",ex="distance_ladder")
n("distance_ladder","The cosmic distance ladder","10",(1e11,1e26),"C2","Astronomy",
 "Astronomers measure distances stepwise: parallax for nearby stars, standard candles like Cepheids for galaxies, then supernovae and redshift for the far universe.",
 "Each rung calibrates the next, so errors propagate upward. Gaia has measured parallaxes for over a billion stars.",
 "d(pc)=1/p(arcsec); m−M=5log₁₀(d/10pc); z→d_L=(1+z)∫c dz/H(z).",
 "Systematics at each rung are debated.",
 em="binary_variable stellar_spectra",ev="Hipparcos and Gaia parallaxes",refs="AST 19; AST 26",fref="hubble_tension",sim="cosmos")
n("milky_way","The Milky Way galaxy","10",(1e19,1e21),"C1","Galactic astronomy",
 "Our galaxy is a spiral of a few hundred billion stars about 100,000 light-years across, with the Sun about 26,000 light-years from the centre.",
 "Stars orbit the centre once every ~230 million years. A supermassive black hole, Sgr A*, sits at the centre.",
 "Disk diameter ≈30 kpc; R☉≈8.2 kpc; v_circ≈230 km/s; M_stars≈6×10¹⁰ M☉; Sgr A* ≈4.3×10⁶ M☉; halo mass ≈10¹² M☉.",
 "Mass of halo depends on dark matter modelling.",
 em="star_formation dark_matter_evidence",ev="Gaia|Stellar orbits around Sgr A*",refs="AST 25",sim="milkyway sgrA")
n("galaxies","Galaxies and the cosmic web","10",(1e19,1e24),"C2","Galactic astronomy",
 "Galaxies are huge collections of stars, gas and dark matter. They come as spirals, ellipticals and irregulars, and are organised into groups, clusters and filaments.",
 "Galaxies form inside dark-matter halos and grow by mergers. Open puzzles include cusp-core and satellite counts, and early galaxies that look surprisingly mature.",
 "Typical L*≈2×10¹⁰ L☉; cluster mass ≈10¹⁴–10¹⁵ M☉; Local Group diameter ≈3 Mpc; filaments tens of Mpc.",
 "Hierarchical picture well supported; small-scale tensions remain.",
 em="gravitation_newton dark_matter_evidence",ev="Hubble Deep Field|Galaxy surveys (SDSS)",refs="AST 26; AST 28",ex="agn_quasars cosmic_web",sim="galaxy localgroup",fr="Cusp-core and missing-satellite problems~open")
n("dark_matter_evidence","Dark matter","10",(1e19,1e26),"C2","Cosmology",
 "Galaxies rotate too fast and clusters bend light too strongly for the visible matter alone, so about 85 % of matter appears to be non-luminous 'dark matter'. What it is remains unknown.",
 "Candidates include WIMPs, axions, sterile neutrinos and primordial black holes. Modified gravity (MOND) fits galaxies but has trouble with clusters and the CMB.",
 "Flat rotation curves v≈const; Ω_cdm h²=0.120; Ω_b h²=0.0224; Bullet Cluster lens-mass offset.",
 "Existence well supported; identity unknown.",
 em="gravitational_lensing galaxies cmb",ev="Rotation curves (Rubin)|Bullet Cluster|CMB peaks",refs="AST 26; AST 28",fref="lz_dark_matter lz_248kev",ex="cosmic_web",fr="Particle identity of dark matter~open|MOND versus particle dark matter~open",sim="galaxy cosmos")
n("agn_quasars","Active galaxies and quasars","10",(1e12,1e24),"C1","Astrophysics",
 "Some galaxies have extremely bright centres powered by gas falling into a supermassive black hole. Quasars outshine whole galaxies.",
 "Jets of plasma travel near light speed. Black-hole mass correlates with the host galaxy's bulge.",
 "L_Edd=1.26×10³¹ W (M/M☉)=4πGMm_pc/σ_T; efficiency η≈0.06–0.4; M-σ relation.",
 "Accretion physics is complex.",
 em="black_holes galaxies",ev="Quasar spectra|EHT M87",refs="AST 27",ex="smbh_growth",sim="blackhole galaxy")
n("smbh_growth","Supermassive black hole growth and early galaxies","10",(1e12,1e24),"C3","Astrophysics",
 "Giant black holes already existed less than a billion years after the Big Bang, which challenges simple growth models. JWST has found compact red sources that may be linked.",
 "Possible routes are heavy seeds from direct collapse, rapid accretion, or mergers of lighter seeds.",
 "Sgr A* 4×10⁶ M☉; quasars at z>7 with M≈10⁹ M☉ within 0.7 Gyr; Salpeter time t_S=450 Myr·η/(1−η).",
 "Open question; measurements of black-hole masses in early objects are uncertain.",
 em="agn_quasars",ev="High-z quasars",refs="AST 27; AST 28",fref="little_red_dots",fr="How early black holes grew so fast~open")
# ---------- cosmology ----------
n("expansion_hubble","Expansion of the universe","11",(1e22,1e27),"C1","Cosmology",
 "Distant galaxies are moving away from us, and the farther they are the faster they go. This is explained by space itself expanding.",
 "Light stretches as space expands, shifting it to longer wavelengths (redshift). The expansion rate today is the Hubble constant.",
 "v=H₀d; 1+z=a₀/a; H₀≈67–74 km/s/Mpc (two methods disagree); Hubble time 1/H₀≈14 Gyr.",
 "Expansion applies on scales beyond bound systems.",
 em="general_relativity distance_ladder",ev="Hubble 1929|Supernova distances",refs="AST 28; AST 29",ex="big_bang hubble_tension_node",fref="hubble_tension",sim="cosmos")
n("hubble_tension_node","The Hubble tension","11",(1e24,1e27),"C3","Cosmology",
 "Two ways of measuring how fast the universe expands, one from the early universe and one from nearby objects, give different answers. Whether this is new physics or measurement error is unresolved.",
 "Proposed fixes include early dark energy or extra relativistic particles, none confirmed.",
 "Planck 2018 ΛCDM: H₀=67.4±0.5 km/s/Mpc; SH0ES Cepheid-SN ladder ≈73 km/s/Mpc (≈5σ gap).",
 "Pre-2024 numbers shown; current status in frontier file.",
 em="expansion_hubble cmb distance_ladder",ev="CMB acoustic peaks|Cepheid/SN ladder",refs="AST 29",fref="hubble_tension",fr="New physics versus unrecognised systematics~open")
n("big_bang","The Big Bang and cosmic history","11",(1e-35,1e27),"C2","Cosmology",
 "The universe began about 13.8 billion years ago in a hot, dense state and has been expanding and cooling since. Atoms formed after about 380,000 years, stars after roughly 100 million years.",
 "Big Bang nucleosynthesis made helium and deuterium in the first few minutes. Dark energy now accelerates the expansion.",
 "Age 13.80 Gyr; t_BBN≈3 min at T≈10⁹ K; recombination z≈1100, 380 kyr; first stars z≈20–30; ΛCDM Ω_Λ≈0.69, Ω_m≈0.31.",
 "Extrapolation back before BBN relies on theory; t<10⁻⁴³ s unknown.",
 em="general_relativity expansion_hubble standard_model",ev="Hubble law|CMB|BBN abundances|Large-scale structure",refs="AST 29; UP3 11",ex="cmb bbn_node inflation cosmic_web reionization",sim="cosmos cmb")
n("cmb","Cosmic microwave background","11",(1e26,1e27),"C1","Cosmology",
 "A faint glow of microwaves from all directions is the cooled light of the early universe, released about 380,000 years after the Big Bang. Its temperature is 2.725 K.",
 "Tiny variations (one part in 100,000) seed galaxies. The pattern of the variations measures the universe's geometry and contents.",
 "T₀=2.7255 K; blackbody to 50 ppm (COBE); ΔT/T≈10⁻⁵; first acoustic peak ℓ≈220 → flat geometry; polarization detected.",
 "Primordial B-modes not detected.",
 em="big_bang blackbody",ev="Penzias-Wilson 1965|COBE|WMAP|Planck",refs="AST 29",ex="dark_matter_evidence",sim="cmb cosmos")
n("bbn_node","Big Bang nucleosynthesis","11",(1e-15,1e-14),"C1","Cosmology",
 "In the first minutes the universe was hot enough to fuse protons and neutrons into helium and traces of deuterium and lithium. Observed abundances match predictions.",
 "The deuterium abundance fixes the density of ordinary matter. Lithium-7 is lower than predicted, an unresolved puzzle.",
 "Y_p≈0.245, D/H≈2.5×10⁻⁵, ³He/H≈10⁻⁵, ⁷Li/H≈5×10⁻¹⁰ (predicted) vs 1.6×10⁻¹⁰ observed.",
 "Well-tested for D and He.",
 em="big_bang nuclear_force",ev="Primordial deuterium in quasar absorption",refs="AST 29; UP3 11",fr="Lithium problem~open")
n("inflation","Cosmic inflation","11",(1e-35,1e27),"C3","Cosmology",
 "A brief period of extremely rapid expansion in the first fraction of a second may explain why the universe is so flat and uniform and how structure began.",
 "Quantum fluctuations stretched to cosmic size become the seeds of galaxies. Signs in gravitational-wave polarisation have not been detected.",
 "N≳60 e-folds; scalar spectral index n_s=0.965±0.004; tensor-to-scalar r<0.036 (95 %).",
 "Not directly confirmed; alternatives exist.",
 em="big_bang quantum_gravity",ev="Flatness|Near scale-invariant spectrum",refs="AST 29",fr="Primordial B-modes not detected~open")
n("dark_energy_node","Dark energy and accelerating expansion","11",(1e25,1e27),"C3","Cosmology",
 "Supernova observations in 1998 showed that the expansion of the universe is speeding up. A mysterious 'dark energy' making up about 68 % of the energy content is the leading explanation.",
 "The simplest version is a cosmological constant, though its observed value is vastly smaller than quantum estimates. Whether it changes over time is being tested.",
 "w=P/ρc²=−1 for Λ; Ω_Λ≈0.69; ρ_Λ≈6×10⁻²⁷ kg/m³; deceleration parameter q₀≈−0.55.",
 "Λ fits all current data; mild hints of evolution are not conclusive.",
 em="expansion_hubble general_relativity",ev="Supernova Hubble diagram (Nobel 2011)|BAO|CMB",refs="AST 29",fref="desi_dark_energy",fr="Nature of dark energy~open|Cosmological-constant problem~open")
n("cosmic_web","Large-scale structure and the cosmic web","11",(1e23,1e26),"C2","Cosmology",
 "Galaxies lie along filaments and walls surrounding huge empty voids. Gravity amplified tiny early fluctuations over billions of years.",
 "Baryon acoustic oscillations leave a standard ruler of ≈150 Mpc in galaxy clustering, used to measure expansion.",
 "BAO scale ≈147 Mpc; σ₈≈0.81; homogeneity scale ≈100 Mpc.",
 "N-body simulations match observations on large scales.",
 em="big_bang dark_matter_evidence",ev="SDSS, DESI maps|Millennium Simulation",refs="AST 28",ex="galaxies",sim="cosmos")
n("reionization","First stars and reionisation","11",(1e22,1e26),"C2","Cosmology",
 "The first stars lit up after about 100–200 million years and their ultraviolet light gradually split the neutral gas between galaxies into ions. By about a billion years the universe was largely reionised.",
 "JWST now sees galaxies from within the first few hundred million years.",
 "Reionisation midpoint z≈7.7±0.7; optical depth τ≈0.054; first stars z≈20–30.",
 "Sources of reionisation still debated.",
 em="big_bang star_formation",ev="Gunn-Peterson troughs|CMB optical depth",refs="AST 28; AST 29",sim="cosmos")
n("fate_multiverse","Fate of the universe and the multiverse","11",(1e26,1e30),"C4","Cosmology",
 "If dark energy stays constant, the universe will keep expanding forever, growing cold and dark. Ideas about other universes (multiverse) are speculative and untested.",
 "Fine-tuning arguments ask why constants allow life; anthropic and multiverse answers are not testable so far.",
 "de Sitter horizon ≈17 Gpc proper; Hawking decay of BHs ≈10⁶⁷–10¹⁰⁰ yr; Λ-dominated ⇒ a∝e^{Ht}.",
 "Speculative extrapolation.",
 em="dark_energy_node inflation",ev="None direct",refs="AST 29",fr="Multiverse is not testable at present~open",sim="cosmos")
n("observable_universe","The observable universe","11",(1e26,1e27),"C1","Cosmology",
 "We can only see as far as light has had time to reach us. The observable universe is about 93 billion light-years across and contains hundreds of billions of galaxies.",
 "Beyond is unseen but probably similar. Because space expands, the edge is farther than 13.8 billion light-years.",
 "Comoving radius ≈14.4 Gpc (46.5 Gly); ≈2×10¹² galaxies (estimate); ≈10⁸⁰ baryons.",
 "Galaxy count estimates uncertain.",
 em="big_bang expansion_hubble",ev="Redshift surveys",refs="AST 29",sim="cosmos")
n("telescopes_tech","Telescopes and astronomical instruments","7",(1e-9,1e27),"C1","Technology",
 "Telescopes gather light to see faint, distant things; radio dishes, X-ray satellites and gravitational-wave detectors let us observe the universe through other channels.",
 "Resolution is limited by diffraction, so bigger mirrors and interferometers see finer detail.",
 "θ≈1.22λ/D; JWST D=6.5 m; EHT baseline ≈ Earth diameter gives ≈20 μas at 1.3 mm; LIGO arms 4 km.",
 "Atmosphere blurs ground-based optical views.",
 em="wave_optics optics_geometric",ev="Space telescope images",refs="AST 6",sim="galaxy")
